.TH OBFUSCATE 1 "2026-09-25" "1.0.0" "Obfuscate Manual"
.SH NAME
obfuscate \- generate a Swift file that obfuscates secrets such as API keys
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B obfuscate
.RB [ \-c ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-k
.IR name = secret "] ..."
.RI [ secret " ...]"
.br
.B obfuscate
.B \-\-version
|
.B \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B obfuscate
generates a Swift source file containing a
.B Secrets
enum, with one property per secret.
Your app reads each secret with
.BI "try Secrets." name \fR,
so the secret never has to appear in your source code or source control.
.PP
Each secret is encrypted with ChaCha20-Poly1305 using its own random key, so every run produces different output.
The generated file never contains a secret in plain text, not even in a comment.
.PP
The generated Swift is written to standard output, so it can be redirected to a file, or written to
.I file
with
.BR \-f .
All other messages, including errors and the results of
.BR \-c ,
go to standard error.
.PP
Reading a secret can throw
.BR Secrets.ObfuscateError ,
although it never should unless the generated file has been edited or corrupted.
The app decides how to handle that error.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-check
Check that every secret decodes correctly before writing any output.
Reports a pass or fail for each secret, by name, on standard error; the secrets themselves are never printed.
If any check fails, nothing is written and
.B obfuscate
exits with status 1.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-file\-name " \fIfile\fR"
Write the generated Swift to
.I file
instead of standard output, adding
.B .swift
if necessary, and report
.RI \(lq "Saved to file" \(rq
on standard error.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key " \fIname\fR=\fIsecret\fR"
A secret, and the name of the
.B Secrets
property that returns it.
The name is everything before the first
.BR = ,
so the secret itself may contain
.BR = .
The name must be a Swift identifier (ASCII letters, digits and underscores, not starting with a digit).
It can't be a Swift keyword,
.BR decrypt ,
.BR ObfuscateError ,
or a name used by another secret.
Repeat for more secrets.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help and exit.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I secret
A secret to obfuscate, typically an API key.
Plain secrets become the properties
.BR apiKey1 ,
.BR apiKey2 ,
and so on, after any secrets given with
.BR \-k .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A check failed, the output file couldn't be written, or a secret couldn't be encrypted.
.TP
.B 64
Invalid usage: no secrets, an invalid
.BR \-k ,
or an unknown option.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B obfuscate \-\-key openAI=sk\-abc123 \-\-key stripe=pk_live_xyz
Print a Secrets file with the properties
.B Secrets.openAI
and
.BR Secrets.stripe .
.TP
.B obfuscate ABCD1234 EFGH5678
Print a Secrets file with the properties
.B Secrets.apiKey1
and
.BR Secrets.apiKey2 .
.TP
.B obfuscate \-c \-f Secrets \-\-key openAI=sk\-abc123
Check that the secret decodes correctly, then write
.BR Secrets.swift .
.TP
.B obfuscate \-\-key openAI=sk\-abc123 > Sources/Secrets.swift
Redirect the generated Swift to a file of your choosing.
.SH CAVEATS
This is obfuscation, not encryption: the key for each secret is in the same file.
It keeps secrets out of plain sight, but anyone motivated enough can recover them.
.PP
Secrets passed on the command line are saved in your shell history, and are briefly visible to other processes, for example in
.BR ps (1).
In
.BR zsh (1),
with
.B setopt HIST_IGNORE_SPACE
set, starting the command with a space keeps it out of the history.
.SH SEE ALSO
The technique is based on NSHipster's article on secret management:
https://nshipster.com/secrets/
